From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 10:29:20 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Add a backend for the Android f_mtp gadget function

Kernels with the android_usb gadget driver, such as the 3.4 kernels still
on phones and tablets, have neither configfs nor a FunctionFS MTP
function: MTP is the driver's own f_mtp, reached through /dev/mtp_usb.
Enable it with usb_f_mtp_mode 1 and point usb_dev_path at the node.

The driver owns the descriptors, the endpoints and the class requests, and
it differs from FunctionFS in ways the MTP code cannot see past:

- Each write() ends a USB transfer, with a ZLP when it was packet aligned.
  A data container written in pieces is therefore gathered and written
  once, and the MTP code's own ZLP is dropped.
- Object data goes through MTP_SEND_FILE_WITH_HEADER and MTP_RECEIVE_FILE,
  the only way to keep one transfer open across the driver's buffers.
- A host Cancel surfaces as ECANCELED, not as a setup request.
- Only one ioctl may run at a time, others fail with EBUSY, and events are
  sent from the inotify thread. Serialize them, or a file transfer loses
  that race and the rest of its data is parsed as MTP commands.
- The driver holds one event and waits a second for the host to read it.
  Many hosts leave the interrupt endpoint unread, so each event would
  stall the next transfer for that second. After a timeout events are
  dropped for 30 s.
- MTP_RECEIVE_FILE cannot recover from a failed file write: it leaves a
  read queued while the host keeps sending, and the rest of the file is
  then parsed as commands. Uploads that will not fit are read and dropped
  instead, and answered with Store Full. If a write fails anyway, umtprd
  exits with an error, since only a new USB session gets the host back in
  step; a service manager can restart it.

The driver's container header has a 32-bit length it does not clamp, so
objects of 4 GB or more cannot be sent to the host.

Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Upstream-Status: Pending
